<!-- Adapted from superpowers by Jesse Vincent (MIT) -->

You are executing `/dev_plan` — turn an approved spec into work items that agents can implement, in parallel where it's safe. This is step 2 of the Feature flow: `/dev_feature` → `/dev_plan` → `/dev_execute`.

## Before starting

1. Read `.docs/architecture.md` and the rules file ({{IF_CLAUDE}}`.claude/rules/rules.md`{{/IF_CLAUDE}}{{IF_COPILOT}}`.github/instructions/rules.instructions.md`{{/IF_COPILOT}}).
2. **Find the TASK** — use the one the user named. Otherwise look in `.docs/todo.md` for TASKs at `specced`: if exactly one, use it; if several, ask which.
3. **Check the status** — it must be `specced`. If it's `brainstorming`, say *"The spec isn't approved yet — run `/dev_feature` first."* and stop. If it's `planned` or `in-progress` and the user asks to revise the plan (or `/dev_execute` sent you here), revise instead: keep items marked ✅, re-derive the rest from the spec, re-check groups and coverage, update Progress, and after approval set the status back to `planned` (nothing done yet) or `in-progress n/m`. If a `plan.md` already exists at `specced`, ask whether to revise it or start over.
4. Read the TASK's `spec.md` in full, then the code its Design section touches.
5. List the project's agents ({{IF_CLAUDE}}`.claude/agents/`{{/IF_CLAUDE}}{{IF_COPILOT}}`.github/chatmodes/`{{/IF_COPILOT}}) — work items are assigned to them.

## Steps

### 1. Derive work items

A work item is one coherent change that an agent can finish and a reviewer can judge on its own. Too small (a single line) wastes a review; too big (a whole subsystem) can't be reviewed well. For each item fill in:

- **Title** — what it delivers, not how.
- **Agent** — the best match from the project's agents (e.g. `backend-developer` for an API endpoint). If none fits, write `main session`.
- **Depends on** — the items whose output this one needs, or `—`.
- **Files** — the exact paths it creates or modifies. Be complete: this list drives parallel safety.
- **Done when** — a concrete check: a command and its expected result, or an observable behavior. *"Works correctly"* is not a check.
- **Steps** — 2–6 short `- [ ]` steps. Include the test step when the project has tests.

### 2. Assign groups

- Order items by dependencies. Items with no unmet dependencies go in group A; items that depend only on group A go in group B; and so on.
- Items in the same group must have **disjoint `Files:` lists**. If two items in a group share a file, move one to the next group.
- Fewer, fuller groups are better: put an item in the earliest group its dependencies allow.

### 3. Check coverage

Fill the Coverage table: every acceptance criterion in the spec maps to at least one work item. If a criterion has no item, add one. If an item serves no criterion, question whether it belongs.

### 4. Write `plan.md`

Copy `.docs/templates/plan.md` into the TASK folder and fill it: the Groups table, the Coverage table, and every work item in the exact format

`### WI-<n> — <title>   [group <X> · agent: <agent-name>]`

followed by the `Depends on:`, `Files:`, `Done when:` lines and the steps. Set **Progress** to `0/<number of items>`.

### 5. Render & open

1. Read `.docs/templates/task-view.html` and the source `.md`.
2. Convert the Markdown to HTML yourself: headings get `id`s (lowercase, hyphenated); tables → `<table>`; fenced code → `<pre><code>` with `<`, `>`, `&` escaped; checkboxes → `☐` / `☑`.
3. Fill `{{TITLE}}` (the doc's H1), `{{SOURCE_FILE}}` (the .md path), `{{TOC}}` (one `<a href="#id">` per H2), `{{CONTENT}}`.
4. Write the result next to the source with the same name and `.html` extension, replacing any previous version.
5. Open it: Windows `cmd /c start "" "<path>"` (works from PowerShell, cmd, and Git Bash), macOS `open "<path>"`, Linux `xdg-open "<path>"`. If the command fails or there is no desktop (SSH, WSL without a browser, container), print the absolute path and continue — never stop the skill over this.

For `plan.html`, also render the Groups section as `<div class="groups">` with one `<div class="group"><h4>Group X</h4>` per group and one `<div class="wi">` per item inside it, with a `<small>` line *agent · depends on*. Add class `done` to items whose heading ends with ✅.

### 6. Approval gate

Ask: *"The plan is open in your browser (`plan.html`): <n> work items in <g> groups. Approve it, or tell me what to change."*

- **Changes** → edit `plan.md`, re-check groups and coverage, re-render, ask again.
- **Approved** → set the todo status to `planned`, then offer: *"Plan approved. Run `/dev_execute` next?"*

## Rules

- Never write or change project code in this skill — it only writes `.docs/` files.
- Never put two items that touch the same file in one group.
- Never set `planned` without the user's explicit approval.
- If planning reveals that the spec is wrong or incomplete, stop and say what's missing — suggest updating the spec with `/dev_feature` rather than planning around it.
- `plan.md` is the source of truth. Never edit `plan.html` by hand; re-render it.
